WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.470
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.919
How about some slow-motion videos!

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:11.710
If we would like to create a slow-motion video
and we don't own an expensive slow-mo camera,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.320
we can try to shoot a normal video and simply
slow it down.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.520
This sounds good on paper, however, the more
we slow it down, the more space we have between

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.710
our individual frames, and at some point,
our video will feel more like a slideshow.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.190
To get around this problem, in a previous
video, we discussed two basic techniques to

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:36.760
fill in these missing frames: one was a naive
technique called frame blending that basically

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.410
computes the average of two images.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.510
In most cases, this doesn't help all that
much because it doesn't have an understanding

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.060
of the motion that takes place in the video.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.070
The other one was optical flow.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:53.649
Now this one is much smarter as it tries to
estimate the kind of translation and rotational

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:58.070
motions that take place in the video and they
typically do much better.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:03.110
However, the disadvantage of this is that
it usually takes forever to compute, and,

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.229
it often introduces visual artifacts.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:11.170
So now, we are going to have a look at NVIDIA's
results, and the main points of interest are

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:16.630
always around the silhouettes of moving objects,
especially around regions where the foreground

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.069
and the background meet.

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:22.420
Keep an eye out for these regions throughout
this video.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.590
For instance, here is one example I've found.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.530
Let me know in the comments section if you
have found more.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.509
This technique builds on U-Net, a superfast
convolutional neural network architecture

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:42.119
that was originally used to segment biomedical
images from limited training data.

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:48.049
This neural network was trained on a bit over
a 1.000 videos and computes multiple approximate

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:53.170
optical flows and combines them in a way that
tries to minimize artifacts.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:57.759
As you see in these side by side comparisons,
it works amazingly well.

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:01.170
Some artifacts still remain, but are often
hard to catch.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.889
And, this architecture blazing fast.

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:09.110
Not real-time yet, but creating a few tens
of these additional frames takes only a few

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.110
seconds.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.850
The quality of the results is also evaluated
and compared to other works in the paper,

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.610
so make sure to have a look!

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.670
As the current, commercially available tools
are super slow and take forever, I cannot

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.730
wait to be able to use this technique to make
some more amazing slow motion footage for

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.000
you Fellow Scholars!

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.530
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

